<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lü Buwei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 291–235 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lü Buwei was a wealthy merchant who became chancellor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">State of Qin</a>. He backed the hostage prince Zichu, who became King Zhuangxiang, and in 249 BCE led the army that annexed the Duchy of Eastern Zhou. He dominated the court during the youth of King Zheng, the future First Emperor, and sponsored the compendium Lüshi Chunqiu, but was dismissed in 237 BCE and took poison two years later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lu-buwei.jpg" alt="Lü Buwei" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 取自清代修《安徽旌德吕氏宗谱》 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%91%82%E4%B8%8D%E9%9F%8B.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The main sources for Lü Buwei date from the first century BCE: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sima-qian">Sima Qian</a>'s Shiji, and Liu Xiang's Zhan Guo Ce and Shuoyuan. Since these Han texts openly criticise both Lü Buwei and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>, some of their stories, such as his private thoughts and conversations, can be discounted. According to the Shiji he came from the state of Wey and became a successful travelling merchant. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/handan">Handan</a>, the capital of Zhao, he met Yiren, a prince of Qin serving there as a hostage, and said, &quot;This is a rare piece of merchandise that should be saved for later.&quot; The Zhan Guo Ce says he turned to politics after reckoning that establishing a ruler would bring an incalculable return.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Using bribes and machinations, Lü Buwei arranged for Yiren to return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> and persuaded Lady Huayang, the childless principal wife of the heir, to adopt him, making him heir apparent. She renamed him Zichu. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a> says Zichu became infatuated with Lady Zhao, a dancing girl of Lü's household, who bore a son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Ying Zheng</a>, in 259 BCE. Zichu succeeded as King Zhuangxiang and made Lü Buwei chancellor and Marquis Wenxin, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of 100,000 households in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luoyang">Luoyang</a>. When Zhuangxiang died in 247 BCE, the 13-year-old Ying Zheng succeeded him, with Lü as regent.</p><p>Lü dominated the government and military and invited scholars from all over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xianyang">Xianyang</a>, where they compiled the Lüshi Chunqiu, an encyclopaedic compendium of the ideas of the Hundred Schools of Thought, published in 239 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Shiji</a>, Lü Buwei, fearing that the Queen Dowager's illicit pursuits would be discovered, introduced to her a retainer, Lao Ai, disguised as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a>. Lao Ai was made Marquis Changxin and took control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> government, and in 238 BCE he launched a revolt that was crushed. Lü Buwei, implicated, was stripped of his posts and titles and banished to Shu, and he took poison, fearing eventual execution.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Ying Zheng</a> then removed power from most of Lü's followers and restored it to the hereditary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> aristocracy. Knoblock and Riegel observe that in the West Lü might be seen as a merchant-prince and patron of culture, a kind of Medici, whereas Han literati, who despised his merchant origins and considered Qin's unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> an evil, condemned him as a parvenu and a fraud.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lü Buwei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The chancellor <b>Lü Buwei</b> dominated the court during the minority of King Zheng, who took personal power in 238 BCE after crushing the revolt of Lao Ai and dismissed Lü Buwei the following year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qin">State of Qin · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 249 BCE Qin's chancellor <b>Lü Buwei</b> led an army that annexed the duchy, extinguishing the last political remnant of the Zhou royal house.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-eastern-zhou">Duchy of Eastern Zhou</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Wey. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-qin">Qin</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
